Act as a senior Roblox developer, level
designer, and horror game director. Help
me build a realistic, terrifying, try-hard
survival horror game in Roblox Studio
using Luau. It must be very hard to
finish, with a huge map and constant
dread. Write real working code, no
placeholders or pseudo code.

GAME CONCEPT
Title: "Ward 13"
Setting: a huge abandoned hospital at
night. 4 floors, a basement morgue, a
rooftop, and a flooded sewer exit.
Realistic, dirty, and decayed, with PBR
materials, grime decals, trash, broken
equipment. No graphic gore, so it
follows Roblox rules.
Players: solo or 1-4 co-op. Monster
speed and difficulty scale with the
number of players.
Story: patients vanished and something
still walks the halls. Tell the story
through notes, audio logs, and the
environment, not cutscenes.

GOAL (5 stages, one life, 30-45 min)
1. Wake up in the basement and find the
   fuse box key.
2. Restore power floor by floor by
   finding 3 hidden fuses.
3. Find 4 keycards (random spawns each
   run) to unlock the exit tunnel.
4. Start the generator and survive a
   60-second lockdown while the monster
   hunts you.
5. Escape through the sewers before the
   water rises.

MAP
Long winding halls, dead ends, locked
shortcuts that open once power is on,
and vents the player can crawl through.
Zones that feel different: morgue,
patient wards, operating theater, kids'
wing, administration, roof, sewers.
Use StreamingEnabled and zone-based
loading so it runs on phones.

MONSTERS
1. The Stalker: main monster. Patrols
   between random waypoints with
   PathfindingService. Hears sprinting,
   slammed doors, and dropped items.
   Sees you with a vision cone and line
   of sight. Chases, searches your last
   known position, checks lockers, then
   slowly gives up. Gets faster and
   smarter every stage.
2. The Crawler: hides in vents and
   ceilings, drops down when you walk
   past.
3. The Watcher: stands far away and only
   moves when you look away. Looking at
   it too long drains sanity.
Always give a sound warning before
danger so deaths feel fair.

STEALTH AND SURVIVAL
- Crouch, sprint with stamina, quiet
  walking.
- Hide in lockers and beds (hold-breath
  minigame).
- Flashlight with a draining battery
  that flickers when the monster is near.
- Sanity meter: low sanity causes fake
  sounds, hallucinations, and fake
  monsters.
- Limited items: batteries, medkits,
  and noise-makers to distract the
  monster. Only 3 inventory slots.
- One life. If you die, the run ends
  and a new randomized run begins.

DIFFICULTY
Hard but fair. Random key, fuse, and
item spawns every run. No checkpoints.
Doors are loud. Resources are tight.
Monster speed rises every stage.

JUMPSCARE DIRECTOR
Build a server-side Director that
tracks player tension and decides when
scares happen. Include lights bursting,
doors slamming, figures in windows,
sudden loud sounds, fake scares that
turn out to be nothing, and real
monster scares. Use cooldowns and
randomness so it is never predictable.
Use silence before big scares. Tension
should rise and fall, not spam.

ATMOSPHERE
Very dark lighting, fog, color
correction, camera shake, flickering
lights, dripping water, distant
footsteps, 3D positional sound with
reverb, a heartbeat that speeds up
when the monster is close, breathing
and whispers, and music that fades to
silence at the scariest moments.

TECH REQUIREMENTS
- All monster, key, item, and win logic
  runs on the server. The client only
  does UI, effects, and input.
- Validate every RemoteEvent.
- Use ModuleScripts and a clean folder
  structure.
- Comment every script.
- Make it work on mobile and low-end
  devices.

HOW TO DELIVER
One system at a time. First give the
full folder structure for Studio
(Workspace, ServerScriptService,
ReplicatedStorage, StarterPlayerScripts,
etc.). Then give full commented Luau
code and say exactly where each script
goes and what type it is (Script,
LocalScript, or ModuleScript). Start
with The Stalker AI. After each system,
tell me how to test it. If something
can't be done in Roblox, tell me and
give the closest alternative. Wait for
me to say "next".
